From: weewx-rtldavis (WeatheredScientist fork)
Subject: rtldavis: drop a frame that is off its transmitter's schedule (time gate)

ROADMAP P1.9 step 4 / #536, DEC-0229.  The decoder accepts any CRC-valid frame
from an active transmitter ID and hops on it at once, with no check of when it
arrived.  A noise decode that passes the CRC by chance (about 1 in 65,536 of some
250 failures a minute) therefore re-anchors the hop clock to a random phase: the
decoder hops blind for 51 misses (143 s) and then parks for up to 149 s, a 2 to 5
minute loss of the station.  Over 30 days of one station's logs, 12 of the 15
garbage frames the driver rejected were followed within seconds by that loss.

A transmitter sends one frame per loop period, and consecutive accepted frames
sit within 0.95 ms (standard deviation) of it.  Once a transmitter is synced, a
frame more than -tolerance ms (default 100; 0 turns the gate off) from the nearest
slot of its schedule is dropped, after the burst gate and before anything is
booked: no re-anchor, no hop, the missed counter stays, nothing reaches the driver.
`offschedule packet: <hex> (<offset> ms)` is logged, and the crc_stats line gains
`offsched=N`.  Init, re-init and undefined IDs have no schedule and are unchanged.

The re-init branch also resets the slot label crc_stats books failures under
(nextHopChan), which used to keep the last blind-hop slot through the park.

tests/test_timegate_patch.py audits every line this patch adds to or removes from
main.go and crcstat.go, runs the gate's Go tests, and replays synthetic frames
through the real Demodulate and Parse.

Applies AFTER rtldavis-dupgate.patch, rtldavis-crcstats.patch and
rtldavis-burstgate.patch, to the rtldavis source bundled in src.tgz.

--- a/timegate/timegate.go
+++ b/timegate/timegate.go
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+package timegate
+
+// Modified by the weewx-rtldavis project (WeatheredScientist): the arrival-time
+// gate, ROADMAP P1.9 step 4 / #536, DEC-0229.
+//
+// A transmitter sends on a fixed schedule: one frame every loop period. The
+// decoder accepts any CRC-valid frame from an active ID and hops on it at once, so
+// a noise decode that passes the CRC by chance puts the hop clock on a random
+// phase, and the station is lost for 2 to 5 minutes. A station cannot transmit off
+// its schedule, so a frame that is off it is not a transmission.
+//
+// The gate decides nothing on its own: main.go asks Off and drops the frame, once
+// the transmitter is synced. Nothing here moves the schedule.
+
+// Offset returns the signed distance in ns from arrival to the nearest slot of the
+// schedule through last with this period. It folds to the nearest slot rather than
+// comparing with the next expected visit because, with more than one transmitter,
+// that visit can already have rolled forward a period; on one transmitter the two
+// agree. With no anchor (last == 0) there is no schedule and the offset is 0.
+func Offset(arrival, last, period int64) int64 {
+	if last == 0 {
+		return 0
+	}
+	d := (arrival - last) % period
+	if d > period/2 {
+		d -= period
+	} else if d < -period/2 {
+		d += period
+	}
+	return d
+}
+
+// Off reports whether offset lies outside tolerance (ns). A tolerance of 0 or less
+// turns the gate off.
+func Off(offset, tolerance int64) bool {
+	if tolerance <= 0 {
+		return false
+	}
+	if offset < 0 {
+		offset = -offset
+	}
+	return offset > tolerance
+}
